---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = 'Is Salesforce a Good Career? The Honest Verdict on Admin, Dev, and Consultant Tracks';
const description =
  'Is Salesforce a good career? Real Admin, Developer, and Consultant pay in India, the Trailhead-to-job-ready path, certification ROI, and where Agentforce genuinely changes the risk.';
const canonical = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-options/is-salesforce-a-good-career';
const publishedDateISO = '2026-08-17';
const publishedDateLabel = '17 August 2026';
const authorName = 'Shivanshi Sehgal';
const authorUrl = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/about/shivanshi-sehgal/';
const authorRole = 'Career researcher and writer';

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: 'short-answer', label: 'The short answer' },
  { id: 'what-is-salesforce-work', label: 'What Salesforce work actually is' },
  { id: 'entry-routes', label: 'How people actually break in' },
  { id: 'track-comparison', label: 'Admin vs Developer vs Consultant vs Architect' },
  { id: 'pay-by-track', label: 'Real pay, track by track' },
  { id: 'certification-check', label: 'The 3-Layer Certification Reality Check' },
  { id: 'salesforce-vs-alternatives', label: 'Salesforce vs other CRM and enterprise-tech tracks' },
  { id: 'ai-agentforce', label: 'Where Agentforce genuinely changes the risk' },
  { id: 'who-fits', label: 'Who this genuinely fits' },
  { id: 'mistakes', label: 'Mistakes that waste the first two years' },
  { id: 'verdict', label: 'The verdict' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQs' },
];

const workRealityComparison = {
  gold: [
    'Most of the job is configuration, process mapping, and stakeholder back-and-forth, not writing code — even Developer roles spend real time reading someone else\'s messy org before touching Apex.',
    'You inherit other people\'s decisions constantly. A production Salesforce org is rarely built cleanly from scratch; it is patched, re-patched, and half-documented by whoever came before you.',
    'The ecosystem moves in three release cycles a year. Staying current is a standing job requirement, not a one-time certification event.',
    'Client and stakeholder patience is thin. Sales and service teams treat the CRM as their livelihood, so a broken flow or a bad deployment gets escalated fast.',
  ],
  teal: [
    'It is one of the few enterprise-tech ecosystems with a genuinely open, free, structured learning path — Trailhead — that a fresher with no CS degree can use to get interview-ready.',
    'Salesforce consulting partners and end-client hiring both run hot in India, so the same skill set opens roles across IT services firms, boutique implementation partners, and in-house teams at large enterprises.',
    'Certifications are recognised and checked by name across the hiring market, which gives a credential-light path a real, verifiable proof point most tech roles do not offer this cleanly.',
    'The pay ceiling for a strong Technical Architect or Solution Architect is genuinely high in India\'s enterprise-software market, well above typical mid-career software or support roles.',
  ],
};

const entryRows = [
  {
    route: 'Trailhead-first, no prior IT job',
    body: 'The most common entry route for career switchers and freshers. Complete Trailhead modules and trails, build 2-3 org configurations in a free Developer org, pass the Administrator certification, then apply for Associate/Junior Admin roles at implementation partners.',
  },
  {
    route: 'IT services lateral move',
    body: 'Someone already working in a service-desk, business-analyst, QA, or general software role inside a company that uses Salesforce (or an IT services firm with a Salesforce practice) moves internally or applies externally once they hold the Admin certification.',
  },
  {
    route: 'Developer background, direct to Platform Developer',
    body: 'Programmers who already know Java, JavaScript, or Apex-adjacent OOP concepts can skip the Admin-first path and go straight for Platform Developer I, since Apex and Lightning Web Components sit closer to their existing skill.',
  },
  {
    route: 'Functional-to-Salesforce consultant switch',
    body: 'Business analysts, ex-sales-ops, or ex-consultants who understand a specific process (sales, service, field service) move into Consultant-track roles by pairing that domain knowledge with certifications like Sales Cloud or Service Cloud Consultant, rather than starting from configuration basics.',
  },
];

const trackComparison = [
  {
    label: 'Administrator',
    title: 'Configuration, workflow automation, user management',
    body: 'Owns the day-to-day org: user setup, security model, Flow automation, reports, dashboards, and keeping the system usable for the sales or service team relying on it. The lowest technical bar of the four tracks and the standard entry point.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Developer',
    title: 'Apex, Lightning Web Components, integrations',
    body: 'Builds what declarative tools cannot: custom Apex logic, Lightning Web Components, API integrations with external systems, and complex data operations. Needs real programming ability, not just platform familiarity.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Consultant',
    title: 'Business process design, client-facing delivery',
    body: 'Sits between the client\'s business problem and the technical build — running discovery, designing the process in Sales Cloud, Service Cloud, or another cloud, and managing the implementation project. Domain and communication skill matter as much as platform depth.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Architect',
    title: 'System design, integration strategy, technical leadership',
    body: 'The senior end of the ladder: designing multi-cloud, multi-system architecture, data models, and integration strategy for large orgs. Usually 5+ years of combined Admin, Developer, and/or Consultant experience before this becomes realistic.',
  },
];

const salaryRows = [
  {
    stage: 'Salesforce Administrator, fresher/junior (0-2 yrs)',
    range: 'Rs 3.5-6.5 LPA',
    reality: 'The most accessible entry point. Pay depends heavily on whether you join an implementation partner (lower start, faster hands-on variety) or an in-house team at a large enterprise (steadier, sometimes higher base).',
  },
  {
    stage: 'Salesforce Developer, junior (0-2 yrs)',
    range: 'Rs 5-9 LPA',
    reality: 'Meaningfully higher than Admin-only roles because Apex and LWC skill is scarcer. Requires genuine coding ability, not just completed Trailhead badges.',
  },
  {
    stage: 'Salesforce Consultant, mid-level (3-5 yrs)',
    range: 'Rs 10-18 LPA',
    reality: 'Pay climbs fastest here once you can run a discovery workshop and own a client relationship, not just configure what someone else designed.',
  },
  {
    stage: 'Salesforce Developer/Consultant, senior (5-8 yrs)',
    range: 'Rs 15-28 LPA',
    reality: 'The range widens a lot by employer type: boutique consulting partners and product companies pay meaningfully more than typical IT-services delivery teams at this stage.',
  },
  {
    stage: 'Technical Architect / Solution Architect (8+ yrs)',
    range: 'Rs 30-55+ LPA',
    reality: 'The genuine ceiling of the ecosystem in India. Requires the CTA (Certified Technical Architect) credential or equivalent deep multi-cloud experience, plus a track record on large, complex implementations.',
  },
];

const certLayers = [
  {
    num: '1',
    title: 'Does the certification match work you can already show, or is it standing alone?',
    body: 'A certification backed by 2-3 real org builds in a free Developer org (a working approval process, a custom object model, a functioning Flow) proves far more to a hiring manager than the badge alone. Recruiters have seen enough "certified but cannot configure a validation rule" candidates to be wary of a certificate with nothing behind it.',
  },
  {
    num: '2',
    title: 'Are you stacking certifications in the order the job market actually hires for, or collecting badges?',
    body: 'Administrator first is correct for almost everyone. After that, the honest next move is the certification that matches the track you are targeting — Platform Developer I for dev roles, Sales Cloud or Service Cloud Consultant for consulting roles — not five unrelated certifications that signal breadth without depth.',
  },
  {
    num: '3',
    title: 'Can you explain a design decision out loud, not just recall the exam answer?',
    body: 'Interviews for real roles ask "why did you choose a Flow over a trigger here" far more than they quiz certification trivia. If you cannot defend a choice you made in your own practice org, the certification has not yet turned into hireable skill.',
  },
];

const alternativesComparison = [
  {
    label: 'Salesforce',
    title: 'Widest hiring pool, most structured free learning path',
    body: 'The largest CRM market share globally and in India means the widest number of implementation partners, in-house teams, and open roles across every track from Admin to Architect. Trailhead is the most mature free learning path of any enterprise platform.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Other CRM platforms (HubSpot, Dynamics 365, Zoho)',
    title: 'Smaller hiring pools, sometimes faster to learn',
    body: 'HubSpot and Zoho skew toward smaller companies and marketing-led teams; Microsoft Dynamics 365 competes directly with Salesforce in large enterprises. All three have real job markets, just noticeably smaller than Salesforce\'s in India\'s IT-services-heavy hiring landscape.',
  },
  {
    label: 'General software development',
    title: 'Broader market, but no platform-specific credential shortcut',
    body: 'A general full-stack or backend developer role has a larger total job market than Salesforce specifically, but lacks a single, universally recognised certification path that lets a non-CS-degree candidate prove readiness as cleanly as Salesforce certifications do.',
  },
  {
    label: 'ERP consulting (SAP, Oracle)',
    title: 'Higher entry bar, comparable or higher senior-level ceiling',
    body: 'SAP and Oracle consulting pay well at the senior end and sometimes exceed Salesforce Architect pay, but entry typically expects a stronger technical or engineering background and a longer, costlier certification path before a first paid role.',
  },
];

const aiComparison = {
  gold: [
    'Agentforce and other AI-agent layers inside Salesforce are already automating a real share of routine configuration suggestions, first-draft Flow logic, and basic case-routing decisions that used to need a junior Admin\'s manual setup.',
    'AI-assisted code generation inside developer tooling is shortening the time to write boilerplate Apex and LWC scaffolding, which is compressing how much billable time a junior Developer can charge for pure code-writing work.',
    'Some routine, high-volume support and configuration tasks that once justified a dedicated junior headcount are being absorbed into AI-assisted workflows at a subset of implementation partners, which is a genuine, if uneven, pressure on the lowest-skill end of Admin hiring.',
  ],
  teal: [
    'Designing the right process for a specific business — what should trigger an escalation, which fields actually matter to a sales team, how a multi-system integration should behave under edge cases — is still a human judgment call that Agentforce assists, not replaces.',
    'Client trust, stakeholder negotiation, and translating a vague business ask into a working system remain squarely human work; no current AI layer runs a discovery workshop or manages a nervous client through a go-live.',
    'The people most exposed are those who only know how to click through standard configuration screens with no underlying process or data-model reasoning. The people least exposed are those who can architect, integrate, and explain trade-offs, which AI tools still cannot reliably do unsupervised.',
  ],
};

const aiStagedPath = [
  { num: '1', title: 'Use Agentforce and AI-assisted tooling from day one, not as an afterthought', body: 'Treat Salesforce\'s own AI features as a required skill layer, the same way Excel became a baseline skill. Employers are actively looking for candidates who can configure and evaluate AI-agent behaviour, not just avoid it.' },
  { num: '2', title: 'Move your own learning from "how do I click this" to "why does this design work"', body: 'The configuration-only layer of Admin work is exactly what AI tooling is starting to compress. Data modelling, security architecture, and integration reasoning are the layers that stay valuable.' },
  { num: '3', title: 'Build one integration or multi-object project, not just single-object CRUD demos', body: 'A project that connects Salesforce to an external system or handles a genuinely messy business process is the kind of proof AI-assisted junior work cannot easily replicate, and it signals architect-track potential early.' },
  { num: '4', title: 'Track which Trailhead and certification content updates with each release', body: 'The ecosystem changes three times a year. Following release notes and updating your practice org after each release is a small habit that keeps a resume from reading as stale within 12-18 months.' },
];

const whoFitsItems = [
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You like solving a business problem more than writing elegant code for its own sake',
    body: 'Salesforce work rewards people who enjoy understanding how a sales team, service desk, or operations team actually works, then building something that fits that reality — not people who only want to write clean algorithms in isolation.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You can sit through a self-paced, structured learning path without external pressure',
    body: 'Trailhead works because it is free and structured, but it also has no built-in accountability. People who finish trails consistently, on their own schedule, usually do well here. People who need a classroom or a cohort to stay disciplined often stall out mid-path.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You are comfortable being client-facing or stakeholder-facing early',
    body: 'Even Admin roles involve explaining a change to a sales manager who does not care about the technical reason, only whether their dashboard works. Consultant and Architect tracks make this constant. People who prefer pure back-end, no-conversation work will find the ecosystem a harder fit than a backend-only developer role.',
  },
];

const whoShouldNotRows = [
  {
    signal: 'You are choosing Salesforce purely because "it pays well" without checking whether configuration and process work interests you',
    reality: 'Admin-track work is repetitive stakeholder-driven configuration most days, not glamorous. People who dislike that daily reality burn out or plateau at Junior Admin regardless of certification count.',
  },
  {
    signal: 'You want to avoid client or stakeholder conversations entirely',
    reality: 'Every track in this ecosystem, including Developer, involves regular back-and-forth with whoever owns the business requirement. A purely heads-down, no-meetings role is a poor match for how Salesforce delivery actually runs.',
  },
  {
    signal: 'You are hoping certifications alone will get you hired with zero hands-on practice',
    reality: 'Hiring managers at implementation partners specifically ask candidates to walk through a build they actually did. A certification with no matching practice org is one of the most common reasons certified candidates still fail interviews.',
  },
  {
    signal: 'You need a large, guaranteed hiring pool right now and cannot wait through a Trailhead-to-job-ready runway',
    reality: 'Going from zero to a credible Junior Admin candidate genuinely takes sustained, consistent effort before the first offer lands — some people move faster, some slower, but there is a real runway, not an instant transition.',
  },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Collecting certifications instead of building a portfolio org',
    body: 'A resume with five certifications and zero demonstrable builds reads as memorised theory to an experienced hiring manager. Two or three well-documented projects in a free Developer org, each solving a real business scenario, outweigh a longer certification list.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Skipping Administrator to jump straight to Developer certifications',
    body: 'Even people with a coding background benefit from understanding the declarative platform first — the data model, security model, and automation tools that every Apex trigger eventually has to respect. Developers who skip this step tend to write Apex for problems Flow could have solved cleanly, which shows up badly in code review.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Treating the first implementation partner job as a permanent home instead of a training ground',
    body: 'Junior roles at implementation partners expose you to more project variety in 18-24 months than a single in-house team often does in years. Staying too long without deliberately building toward a Consultant or senior Developer track can flatten your growth curve.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Ignoring release notes and letting skills go stale',
    body: 'The platform ships three release cycles a year. Candidates who have not touched a new feature since their original certification exam start losing credibility in technical interviews within about 12-18 months.',
  },
  {
    num: '05',
    title: 'Never learning to explain a design trade-off out loud',
    body: 'The jump from Admin to Consultant, and from Developer to Architect, depends on being able to justify a decision to a client or a technical lead, not just execute it. Practising this explicitly — in mock interviews, internal reviews, or even writing it down — is what actually unlocks the next pay band.',
  },
];

const verdictYes = [
  'You genuinely enjoy mapping a business process and building a system that fits it, more than writing algorithms in isolation.',
  'You can sustain self-paced learning through Trailhead without needing external classroom pressure to stay consistent.',
  'You are comfortable being client-facing or stakeholder-facing from early in your career, not just in senior roles.',
  'You want a credential-verifiable path into enterprise tech without needing a CS degree or years of prior coding experience first.',
]

const verdictNo = [
  'You are picking Salesforce only because of salary screenshots, without checking whether configuration-heavy, stakeholder-driven work actually interests you.',
  'You want to avoid client and stakeholder conversations, at any career stage, including the early years.',
  'You are hoping to collect certifications with no matching hands-on practice and expect that alone to get you hired.',
  'You need an instant transition with no runway — Trailhead-to-job-ready takes real, sustained effort before a credible first offer.',
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Is Salesforce a good career in India?',
    a: 'Yes, for people who genuinely enjoy configuring systems around real business processes and are willing to build hands-on proof alongside certifications, not just collect badges. Salesforce has the widest hiring pool of any single CRM platform in India\'s IT-services and enterprise market, a free structured learning path in Trailhead, and a certification system that hiring managers actually check by name. It is a weaker fit for someone who wants to avoid client-facing work or expects certifications alone to guarantee a job with no practice.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need a computer science degree to become a Salesforce Administrator or Developer?',
    a: 'No. Salesforce Administrator roles do not require a CS degree — the Trailhead learning path and Administrator certification are built specifically to be accessible to career switchers, business-background graduates, and freshers from any stream. Developer roles need genuine programming ability (Apex is Java-like), which is learnable through Trailhead\'s developer trails and practice, but a formal CS degree is not a hard requirement the way it can be for some pure software-engineering roles.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does it realistically take to become job-ready in Salesforce?',
    a: 'There is no fixed timeline — some people move faster with more time to dedicate, others need longer, and both are normal. The realistic path is: complete the core Trailhead trails, build 2-3 real projects in a free Developer org, pass the Administrator certification, then start applying while continuing to build. Treat certification-only readiness with suspicion; hiring managers consistently value a documented practice org over a certificate with no build behind it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which Salesforce track pays the most: Admin, Developer, Consultant, or Architect?',
    a: 'Architect roles (Technical Architect, Solution Architect) sit at the top of the ecosystem\'s pay ceiling in India, commonly reaching Rs 30-55+ LPA at senior levels, but they require 8+ years of combined experience across the other tracks first. Among entry-to-mid-level tracks, Developer and Consultant roles typically out-earn pure Administrator roles once you clear the first 2-3 years, because Apex/LWC skill and client-facing delivery skill are both scarcer than configuration-only Admin skill.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will AI and Agentforce replace Salesforce Admin and Developer jobs?',
    a: 'AI-assisted tools inside Salesforce, including Agentforce, are already automating some routine configuration suggestions and boilerplate code generation, which is a real and specific pressure on the lowest-skill, purely click-through layer of Admin work and the most repetitive layer of junior Developer coding. It is not close to replacing the judgment-heavy work of designing a process for a specific business, running client discovery, or architecting an integration across systems. The safer long-term position is learning to configure and evaluate AI-agent behaviour as a core skill, not avoiding it, while building depth in data modelling and system design that AI tools still cannot reliably do unsupervised.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the Salesforce Administrator certification worth it without work experience?',
    a: 'It is worth it as a credible, verifiable first proof point, but only when it is backed by real hands-on practice in a free Developer org, not treated as a standalone credential. A certification with 2-3 documented real-scenario builds behind it is a genuinely strong entry signal for a fresher with no prior IT job; a certification with zero practice behind it is one of the most common reasons certified candidates still fail interviews.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};

const relatedGuides = [
  { href: '/blog/career-options/is-sales-a-good-career', label: 'Is sales a good career? The pure sales-track verdict, for comparison' },
  { href: '/blog/career-options/devops-roadmap-india', label: 'DevOps roadmap India: another certification-driven enterprise-tech entry path' },
];
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Is Salesforce a good career? Yes, if you genuinely enjoy mapping business processes and
    building systems around them, and you are willing to back certifications with real hands-on
    practice — and no, if you are chasing it purely for salary screenshots or hoping a badge alone
    gets you hired.</strong>
    Salesforce is one of the few enterprise-tech ecosystems where a free, structured learning path —
    Trailhead — plus a verifiable certification can take someone with no CS degree from zero to a
    credible first offer. Turning that into an actual
    <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> — real projects in a practice org, not just completed
    modules — is what separates people who build genuine <strong>high income opportunities</strong> in
    this ecosystem from people who collect badges and still cannot get shortlisted.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Salesforce genuinely fits people who like process-and-systems thinking, can self-manage through Trailhead's structured but unsupervised learning path, and are comfortable being client-facing early.</li>
      <li>No CS degree is required for Administrator or most Consultant-track roles; Developer roles need real programming ability, learnable but not automatic.</li>
      <li>Fresher pay ranges by track: roughly Rs 3.5-6.5 LPA for Admin, Rs 5-9 LPA for Developer, climbing to Rs 30-55+ LPA at Technical/Solution Architect level with 8+ years in.</li>
      <li><strong>Run every certification through the 3-Layer Certification Reality Check below before treating it as job-ready proof.</strong></li>
      <li>Agentforce and AI-assisted tooling are already compressing the most routine, click-through layer of configuration and boilerplate coding — not the process-design, client-discovery, or architecture layer, which still needs a human.</li>
      <li>Building a genuine high-value skill portfolio here — documented projects, not just badges — is what compounds toward Consultant and Architect-level income over time.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you are weighing this against a bigger decision — which stream, which platform, or whether tech
    is the right direction at all —
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career
    guidance</a> can help you compare it against your own numbers instead of a generic pros-and-cons list.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is Salesforce a good career"</h2>

  <p>
    Salesforce is a real, durable enterprise-tech career field in India, not a shortcut trend or a
    resume filler. It is the world's most widely used CRM platform, and its India hiring market spans
    IT services giants, boutique implementation partners, and in-house teams at large enterprises.
  </p>

  <p>
    But like most credential-accessible tech paths, the honest answer depends on whether you actually
    do the hands-on work behind the certification, not just the certification exam itself. Salesforce
    pays for demonstrated ability to configure, build, and explain a working system — not for a
    certificate with nothing behind it.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Most "is Salesforce a good career" content either sells the "$50,000 average salary" American
      stat with no India context, or reduces the whole ecosystem to "get certified, get hired" without
      mentioning how many certified candidates still fail interviews because they cannot explain a
      design choice. The real answer needs your actual interest in process-and-systems work, your
      ability to self-study a structured but unsupervised path, and whether you are building projects
      alongside the badges — not just the badges.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what-is-salesforce-work">What Salesforce work actually is</h2>

  <p>
    "Salesforce career" covers a wide range of daily work, and the glamorous parts — architecting a
    multi-cloud system, presenting to a client — are a small share of most people's actual week,
    especially early on.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">The daily reality</div>
      <ul>
        {workRealityComparison.gold.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What genuinely works in its favour</div>
      <ul>
        {workRealityComparison.teal.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Neither column is the full story alone. The decision is whether the left column's process-heavy,
    stakeholder-driven daily work is something you would actually enjoy for years, in exchange for the
    right column's real accessibility and pay ceiling.
  </p>

  <h2 id="entry-routes">How people actually break into Salesforce</h2>

  <p>
    Unlike a CS-degree-gated software engineering role, Salesforce has a genuinely open front door.
    That openness is real, but it also means the ecosystem sees a lot of people who complete Trailhead
    modules passively without turning them into hireable proof.
  </p>

  <div class="stage-stack">
    {entryRows.map((item) => (
      <div class="stage-row">
        <div class="stage-row__level">{item.route}</div>
        <div class="stage-row__body">
          <p class="stage-row__subjects">{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Because the entry bar looks deceptively simple — "just get certified" — it is easy to collect
      badges without ever building the practice-org proof that hiring managers actually screen for.
      The certification check further down this page exists specifically to catch that gap before you
      hit the job market.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="track-comparison">Admin vs Developer vs Consultant vs Architect</h2>

  <p>
    "Salesforce job" is not one role. Picking the right track deliberately, based on what kind of work
    you actually want to do day to day, matters more than picking the track with the highest headline
    salary number.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid feature-grid--quad">
    {trackComparison.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    Most people move through these in sequence rather than picking one for life: Admin skills underpin
    Developer and Consultant work, and Architect roles need real time in at least two of the earlier
    three tracks first. Someone comparing this against a pure sales-track career, where the skill set
    and daily reality are entirely different, can see that contrast directly in
    <a href="/blog/career-options/is-sales-a-good-career">is sales a good career</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pay-by-track">Real pay, track by track</h2>

  <p>
    A single "Salesforce salary in India" number hides more than it reveals, because pay swings hugely
    by track, seniority, and whether you join an implementation partner, an IT services firm, or an
    in-house enterprise team.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Track, stage</th>
          <th>Typical range</th>
          <th>Reality</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {salaryRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
