      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    Treat any claim that AI makes a Salesforce career "10x more valuable" or "completely future-proof"
    with the same skepticism you would apply to any single-tool promise — the honest picture is
    narrower and more specific than either extreme. The disruption risk is real but concentrated in
    the routine, click-through configuration layer and the most repetitive boilerplate-coding layer.
    It has not reached process design, client discovery, or system architecture, which is exactly why
    those are the skills worth deliberately building toward, not the ones AI tooling is currently
    compressing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-fits">Who this genuinely fits, and who should look elsewhere</h2>

  <p>
    Salesforce rewards more than "good with computers." The people who go furthest pair genuine
    interest in process-and-systems thinking with the discipline to self-study a structured but
    unsupervised learning path, and comfort being client-facing well before senior levels.
  </p>

